traces.inference_trace:
Walk through the array once and remember the index of the most recent `1` (start with -1 meaning none seen yet).

- When we hit a `1` at index `i` and a previous `1` exists at index `last`, the number of zeros between them is `i - last - 1`.
- If that count is less than `k`, the two `1`s are too close, so return `false`.
- Otherwise update `last = i` and continue.
- If the loop finishes without a violation, return `true`.

This runs in O(n) time and O(1) extra space.

```typescript
function kLengthApart(nums: number[], k: number): boolean {
    let last = -1;
    for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
        if (nums[i] === 1) {
            if (last !== -1 && i - last - 1 < k) return false;
            last = i;
        }
    }
    return true;
};
```

