traces.inference_trace:
Sort the array so every possible child value is processed before its parent. Let `dp[x]` be the number of trees with root `x`.

1. Start each `dp[i]` at 1, for the single-node tree.
2. For each `arr[i]`, look at every smaller `arr[j]`. If `arr[j]` divides `arr[i]` and the quotient `q` is also in the array, then `arr[j]` and `q` can be the two children. That adds `dp[j] * dp[q]` trees to `dp[i]`.
3. Ordered pairs are counted automatically, because `j` runs over both children. For example, `10 = 2 * 5` and `10 = 5 * 2` are counted separately, as the problem requires.
4. The answer is the sum of all `dp[i]`, modulo 1e9+7.

Overflow: each `dp` value is below 1e9+7, so a product can reach about 1e18. That exceeds the exact-integer range of a JS number, so the multiplication uses `BigInt`.

Complexity: there are about 5e5 inner-loop iterations. Only the iterations where `arr[j]` divides `arr[i]` do a `BigInt` multiply, so this is fast enough.

```typescript
function numFactoredBinaryTrees(arr: number[]): number {
    const MOD = 1000000007n;
    const a = [...arr].sort((x, y) => x - y);
    const n = a.length;
    const idx = new Map<number, number>();
    a.forEach((v, i) => idx.set(v, i));
    const dp: bigint[] = new Array(n).fill(1n);
    let total = 0n;
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        let s = 1n;
        for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {
            if (a[i] % a[j] === 0) {
                const q = a[i] / a[j];
                const k = idx.get(q);
                if (k !== undefined) {
                    s = (s + dp[j] * dp[k]) % MOD;
                }
            }
        }
        dp[i] = s;
        total = (total + s) % MOD;
    }
    return Number(total);
};
```

