Take the first two points as the reference line. Every other point must lie on that line.

For the first point (x0, y0), let dx = x1 − x0 and dy = y1 − y0, using the second point (x1, y1). A point (x, y) is collinear with the first two exactly when the cross product is zero:

(x − x0) · dy − (y − y0) · dx = 0

Using the cross product instead of slopes avoids division, so vertical lines need no special case.

The coordinates are bounded by 10^4, so each product stays well within safe integer range.

```typescript
function checkStraightLine(coordinates: number[][]): boolean {
    const [x0, y0] = coordinates[0];
    const [x1, y1] = coordinates[1];
    const dx = x1 - x0, dy = y1 - y0;
    for (let i = 2; i < coordinates.length; i++) {
        const [x, y] = coordinates[i];
        if ((x - x0) * dy - (y - y0) * dx !== 0) return false;
    }
    return true;
};
```

