Compute `f` for every word and query. Since each string has length at most 10, `f` is always between 1 and 10.

1. Build a count array `cnt`, where `cnt[k]` is the number of words with `f(W) = k`.
2. Build a suffix sum `suf`, where `suf[k]` is the number of words with `f(W) >= k`.
3. For each query `q`, the answer is `suf[f(q) + 1]`, the number of words whose `f` is strictly greater.

The examples contain trailing spaces, so `f` only counts characters between `'a'` and `'z'` and ignores anything else.

```typescript
function numSmallerByFrequency(queries: string[], words: string[]): number[] {
    const f = (s: string): number => {
        let minC = 200, cnt = 0;
        for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {
            const c = s.charCodeAt(i);
            if (c < 97 || c > 122) continue;
            if (c < minC) { minC = c; cnt = 1; }
            else if (c === minC) cnt++;
        }
        return cnt;
    };
    const cnt = new Array(13).fill(0);
    for (const w of words) {
        const v = f(w);
        cnt[Math.min(v, 11)]++;
    }
    const suf = new Array(14).fill(0);
    for (let k = 12; k >= 0; k--) suf[k] = suf[k + 1] + cnt[k];
    return queries.map(q => {
        const v = Math.min(f(q), 11);
        return suf[v + 1];
    });
};
```

