For each anchor point i, count how many later points fall on each line through i, keyed by a normalized slope.

1. For every j > i, compute dx and dy between point j and point i.
2. Divide both by their gcd so equal slopes produce identical pairs.
3. Normalize the sign: if dx < 0, or dx == 0 and dy < 0, negate both. Then opposite directions map to the same key.
4. Use the key "dx,dy" in a map and count occurrences. The best line through i has max count + 1 points (the anchor itself).
5. All points are unique, so dx and dy are never both zero.
6. With n ≤ 300, this is O(n² log C), which is fast enough.

```typescript
function maxPoints(points: number[][]): number {
    const n = points.length;
    if (n <= 2) return n;
    const gcd = (a: number, b: number): number => {
        a = Math.abs(a); b = Math.abs(b);
        while (b) { const t = a % b; a = b; b = t; }
        return a;
    };
    let best = 1;
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        const map = new Map<string, number>();
        let localMax = 0;
        for (let j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
            let dx = points[j][0] - points[i][0];
            let dy = points[j][1] - points[i][1];
            const g = gcd(dx, dy);
            dx /= g; dy /= g;
            if (dx < 0 || (dx === 0 && dy < 0)) { dx = -dx; dy = -dy; }
            const key = dx + "," + dy;
            const c = (map.get(key) || 0) + 1;
            map.set(key, c);
            if (c > localMax) localMax = c;
        }
        if (localMax + 1 > best) best = localMax + 1;
    }
    return best;
};
```

