The maximum bitwise OR of any subset is the OR of the entire array, since adding elements can never clear a bit. So the task reduces to counting subsets whose OR equals that value.

With `nums.length <= 16`, there are at most 2^16 = 65,536 subsets, so we can check every one.

To avoid recomputing each subset's OR from scratch, use a DP over bitmasks:
- For a mask `m`, let `lowbit = m & -m` be its lowest set bit.
- Then `or[m] = or[m ^ lowbit] | nums[index of lowbit]`.
- The index of `lowbit` is `31 - Math.clz32(lowbit)`.

We fill `or[]` for all masks from 1 to 2^n − 1 and count how many equal the full-array OR. Total work is O(2^n).

```typescript
function countMaxOrSubsets(nums: number[]): number {
    const n = nums.length;
    let maxOr = 0;
    for (const x of nums) maxOr |= x;
    const total = 1 << n;
    const or = new Int32Array(total);
    let count = 0;
    for (let m = 1; m < total; m++) {
        const low = m & -m;
        const idx = 31 - Math.clz32(low);
        or[m] = or[m ^ low] | nums[idx];
        if (or[m] === maxOr) count++;
    }
    return count;
};
```

