Treat each slot as having a fill count of 0, 1 or 2, and encode the fill counts of all slots as one base-3 number. The total number of states is 3^numSlots ≤ 19683.

For any state, the number of items already placed equals the sum of its base-3 digits. Call that `i`. The next item to place is `nums[i]`.

Let `dp[mask]` be the best AND sum reachable in state `mask`:

- Start with `dp[0] = 0` and every other entry at -1 (unreachable).
- Iterate `mask` in increasing order. Every transition adds `3^j` to the mask, so states are always processed before the states they lead to.
- For a reachable `mask` with `i < n`, try each slot `j` whose digit is below 2. Update `dp[mask + 3^j]` with `dp[mask] + (nums[i] & (j+1))`.
- The answer is the maximum `dp[mask]` over states whose digit sum equals `n`.

The work is about 19683 × 9 operations, which is negligible.

```typescript
function maximumANDSum(nums: number[], numSlots: number): number {
    const n = nums.length;
    const pow: number[] = [1];
    for (let j = 1; j <= numSlots; j++) pow.push(pow[j - 1] * 3);
    const total = pow[numSlots];
    const dp = new Int32Array(total).fill(-1);
    dp[0] = 0;
    let ans = 0;
    for (let mask = 0; mask < total; mask++) {
        if (dp[mask] < 0) continue;
        // compute count
        let cnt = 0;
        let m = mask;
        const digits: number[] = [];
        for (let j = 0; j < numSlots; j++) {
            const d = m % 3;
            digits.push(d);
            cnt += d;
            m = (m - d) / 3;
        }
        if (cnt === n) {
            if (dp[mask] > ans) ans = dp[mask];
            continue;
        }
        if (cnt > n) continue;
        const v = nums[cnt];
        for (let j = 0; j < numSlots; j++) {
            if (digits[j] < 2) {
                const nm = mask + pow[j];
                const val = dp[mask] + (v & (j + 1));
                if (val > dp[nm]) dp[nm] = val;
            }
        }
    }
    return ans;
};
