Approach: scan prefixes left to right. Keep `cnt[x]`, the number of times value `x` has appeared, and `freq[f]`, how many distinct values currently appear exactly `f` times. Track `maxF`, the highest count of any value so far.

For a prefix of length `len = i + 1`, removing exactly one element works in one of three cases:

1. **Every value appears once** (`maxF == 1`). Remove any element; the rest still all appear once.
2. **One value has count `maxF`, all others have `maxF - 1`.** This means `freq[maxF] == 1` and `maxF + freq[maxF-1] * (maxF - 1) == len`. Removing one copy of the top value equalizes everything.
3. **One value appears once, all others have `maxF`.** This means `freq[maxF] * maxF + 1 == len`. Removing the single element leaves every value at `maxF`.

Case 2 also covers a prefix with only one distinct value: `freq[maxF-1]` is 0, so the check reduces to `maxF == len`.

The answer is the largest `len` that satisfies any case. Each step is O(1), so the whole scan is O(n).

```typescript
function maxEqualFreq(nums: number[]): number {
    const cnt = new Int32Array(100002);
    const freq = new Int32Array(100002);
    let maxF = 0;
    let ans = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
        const x = nums[i];
        if (cnt[x] > 0) freq[cnt[x]]--;
        cnt[x]++;
        freq[cnt[x]]++;
        if (cnt[x] > maxF) maxF = cnt[x];
        const len = i + 1;
        if (
            maxF === 1 ||
            (freq[maxF] === 1 && maxF + freq[maxF - 1] * (maxF - 1) === len) ||
            (freq[maxF] * maxF + 1 === len)
        ) {
            ans = len;
        }
    }
    return ans;
};
```

