Use two pointers: `lo` starts at 0 and `hi` starts at n.

- For each character, if it is `'I'`, output `lo` and increment it. Every number placed later is larger than `lo`, so the next element is guaranteed to be greater.
- If it is `'D'`, output `hi` and decrement it. Every number placed later is smaller than `hi`, so the next element is guaranteed to be smaller.
- After processing all n characters, `lo == hi`, so append that last remaining value.

This runs in O(n) time.

```typescript
function diStringMatch(s: string): number[] {
    const n = s.length;
    let lo = 0, hi = n;
    const res: number[] = new Array(n + 1);
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (s[i] === 'I') {
            res[i] = lo++;
        } else {
            res[i] = hi--;
        }
    }
    res[n] = lo;
    return res;
};
```

