Approach: scan the array once and, for every index `j` where `nums[j] == key`, add the window of indices `[j - k, j + k]` (clipped to the array bounds) to the result.

- Track `next`, the smallest index not yet added to the result.
- For each key position `j`, start the window at `max(next, j - k)` so no index is added twice.
- End the window at `min(n - 1, j + k)`, then set `next` to one past the window end.
- Key positions are processed left to right, so indices are appended in increasing order and the result is already sorted.

Each index is added at most once, so the total time is O(n).

```typescript
function findKDistantIndices(nums: number[], key: number, k: number): number[] {
    const n = nums.length;
    const res: number[] = [];
    let next = 0;
    for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) {
        if (nums[j] === key) {
            const start = Math.max(next, j - k);
            const end = Math.min(n - 1, j + k);
            for (let i = start; i <= end; i++) res.push(i);
            next = Math.max(next, end + 1);
        }
    }
    return res;
};
```

