Approach: reverse topological sort (Kahn's algorithm), which avoids recursion depth issues.

1. Build the reverse graph: for every edge `i -> j`, record `j -> i`. Let `outdeg[i] = graph[i].length`.
2. Push every node with out-degree 0 (the terminal nodes) onto a queue.
3. Pop a node `v` and mark it safe. For each predecessor `u` of `v`, decrement `outdeg[u]`. When `outdeg[u]` reaches 0, all of `u`'s outgoing edges lead to safe nodes, so push `u`.
4. Nodes on a cycle, or that can reach a cycle, never reach out-degree 0, so they are never marked safe.
5. Collect the safe nodes by scanning indices in order, which gives ascending output.

This runs in O(n + e) time and space.

```typescript
function eventualSafeNodes(graph: number[][]): number[] {
    const n = graph.length;
    const rev: number[][] = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
    const outdeg = new Array(n).fill(0);
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        outdeg[i] = graph[i].length;
        for (const j of graph[i]) rev[j].push(i);
    }
    const queue: number[] = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (outdeg[i] === 0) queue.push(i);
    const safe = new Array(n).fill(false);
    let head = 0;
    while (head < queue.length) {
        const v = queue[head++];
        safe[v] = true;
        for (const u of rev[v]) {
            outdeg[u]--;
            if (outdeg[u] === 0) queue.push(u);
        }
    }
    const res: number[] = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (safe[i]) res.push(i);
    return res;
};
```

