Use patience sorting. Keep an array `tails`, where `tails[k]` is the smallest possible tail value of any strictly increasing subsequence of length `k + 1` seen so far. This array is always sorted, so each element can be placed with a binary search.

For each number `x`:
- Binary search for the first index whose tail is `>= x` (lower bound).
- If no such index exists, `x` extends the longest subsequence, so append it.
- Otherwise, replace that tail with `x`, which keeps the tail as small as possible.

Using a lower bound (`>=`) rather than an upper bound keeps the subsequence strictly increasing, so equal values never extend a run.

The answer is the length of `tails`. The total time is O(n log n).

```typescript
function lengthOfLIS(nums: number[]): number {
    const tails: number[] = [];
    for (const x of nums) {
        let lo = 0, hi = tails.length;
        while (lo < hi) {
            const mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;
            if (tails[mid] < x) lo = mid + 1;
            else hi = mid;
        }
        if (lo === tails.length) tails.push(x);
        else tails[lo] = x;
    }
    return tails.length;
};
```

