Approach: dynamic programming over words processed from shortest to longest.

1. Sort the words by length, so every possible predecessor is handled before the words it can extend.
2. For each word, try deleting each one of its characters in turn. Each deletion gives a candidate predecessor.
3. Keep a map from word to the length of the longest chain ending at that word. The chain length for the current word is 1 plus the best value found among its candidate predecessors. If no predecessor exists in the map, the value is 1.
4. Track the maximum value seen and return it.

Duplicate words are handled correctly: a word cannot be its own predecessor, because every candidate is one character shorter, so storing the max for each word is enough.

Complexity: sorting costs O(n log n). Each word has at most 16 deletions, and each deletion builds a string of length up to 16, so the main loop is O(n · L²) with L ≤ 16.

```typescript
function longestStrChain(words: string[]): number {
    const sorted = [...words].sort((a, b) => a.length - b.length);
    const dp = new Map<string, number>();
    let best = 0;
    for (const w of sorted) {
        let cur = 1;
        for (let i = 0; i < w.length; i++) {
            const prev = w.slice(0, i) + w.slice(i + 1);
            const v = dp.get(prev);
            if (v !== undefined && v + 1 > cur) cur = v + 1;
        }
        const existing = dp.get(w) ?? 0;
        if (cur > existing) dp.set(w, cur);
        if (cur > best) best = cur;
    }
    return best;
};
```

