The answer is always 0, 1 or 2:

1. **Answer 0:** if the number of islands is not exactly 1, the grid is already disconnected.
2. **Answer 1:** if removing some single land cell makes the island count differ from 1. That happens when the cell is an articulation point, or when it is the only land cell (removing it leaves 0 islands).
3. **Answer 2:** otherwise. Any island with at least 2 cells has a corner-like cell that can be isolated by removing at most 2 neighbors, so 2 days always suffice.

**Cost of brute force:** the grid is at most 30x30 = 900 cells. Trying every land cell and recounting islands costs about 900 × 900 ≈ 810k operations, which is fine.

**Counting islands:** use an iterative flood fill with an explicit stack to avoid recursion-depth problems.

```typescript
function minDays(grid: number[][]): number {
    const m = grid.length, n = grid[0].length;
    const count = (): number => {
        const seen: boolean[] = new Array(m * n).fill(false);
        let c = 0;
        const stack: number[] = [];
        for (let i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                if (grid[i][j] === 1 && !seen[i * n + j]) {
                    c++;
                    seen[i * n + j] = true;
                    stack.push(i * n + j);
                    while (stack.length) {
                        const cur = stack.pop()!;
                        const r = Math.floor(cur / n), col = cur % n;
                        const dirs = [[1, 0], [-1, 0], [0, 1], [0, -1]];
                        for (const [dr, dc] of dirs) {
                            const nr = r + dr, nc = col + dc;
                            if (nr >= 0 && nr < m && nc >= 0 && nc < n && grid[nr][nc] === 1 && !seen[nr * n + nc]) {
                                seen[nr * n + nc] = true;
                                stack.push(nr * n + nc);
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        return c;
    };
    if (count() !== 1) return 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < m; i++) {
        for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) {
            if (grid[i][j] === 1) {
                grid[i][j] = 0;
                const c = count();
                grid[i][j] = 1;
                if (c !== 1) return 1;
            }
        }
    }
    return 2;
};
```

