Sort the array in descending order and take the largest elements one at a time. Stop as soon as the running sum of taken elements is strictly greater than the sum of the remaining elements.

Taking the largest values first reaches the target with the fewest elements. Among subsets of that size, it also gives the maximum total sum. The result is already in non-increasing order because we built it from the sorted array.

```typescript
function minSubsequence(nums: number[]): number[] {
    const sorted = [...nums].sort((a, b) => b - a);
    const total = sorted.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
    const res: number[] = [];
    let sum = 0;
    for (const x of sorted) {
        sum += x;
        res.push(x);
        if (sum > total - sum) break;
    }
    return res;
};
```

