Use a monotonic stack and walk the array twice to handle the circular wrap.

1. Initialize `res` with `-1` for every position, and keep a stack of indices.
2. Iterate `i` from `0` to `2n - 1`, and let `idx = i % n`.
3. While the stack is non-empty and the element at the top index is smaller than `nums[idx]`, pop that index and set its answer to `nums[idx]`. This value is the first greater element seen after it.
4. Push `idx` only during the first pass (`i < n`). The second pass exists only to resolve elements that need to look past the end of the array.

Any index left on the stack has no greater element, so its answer stays `-1`.

Each index is pushed and popped at most once, so the time is O(n) and the space is O(n).

```typescript
function nextGreaterElements(nums: number[]): number[] {
    const n = nums.length;
    const res: number[] = new Array(n).fill(-1);
    const stack: number[] = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < 2 * n; i++) {
        const idx = i % n;
        const v = nums[idx];
        while (stack.length > 0 && nums[stack[stack.length - 1]] < v) {
            res[stack.pop()!] = v;
        }
        if (i < n) stack.push(idx);
    }
    return res;
};
```

