For each pair (j, k) with j < k in one array, count how many values in the other array have a square equal to `nums[j] * nums[k]`.

1. Build a frequency map of the squares of the other array. Each value is at most 1e5, so a square is at most 1e10, which is exactly representable in a JS number.
2. For every pair (j, k) with j < k, look up the product `nums[j] * nums[k]` in that map and add its count.
