Build a map from each value to the list of indices where it appears. This takes O(n) time once, in the constructor.

Each `pick(target)` call then looks up the target's index list and returns a uniformly random element from it, in O(1) time. Since every index in the list is equally likely to be chosen, each valid index has equal probability.

```typescript
class Solution {
    private map: Map<number, number[]>;

    constructor(nums: number[]) {
        this.map = new Map();
        for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            const v = nums[i];
            let arr = this.map.get(v);
            if (!arr) {
                arr = [];
                this.map.set(v, arr);
            }
            arr.push(i);
        }
    }

    pick(target: number): number {
        const arr = this.map.get(target)!;
        return arr[Math.floor(Math.random() * arr.length)];
    }
}
```

