Sort the products, then for each prefix of `searchWord` binary-search for the first product that is `>=` the prefix. Because the list is sorted, any matches start there and are contiguous, so we check up to the next three entries and keep those that start with the prefix.

For lowercase letters, plain JS `<` string comparison is lexicographic, so the default `sort()` gives the right order.

Each prefix costs O(log n) comparisons for the search, each comparison up to O(L), plus a small constant amount of work to collect at most three matches. This is fast enough for the given constraints.

```typescript
function suggestedProducts(products: string[], searchWord: string): string[][] {
    const sorted = products.slice().sort();
    const n = sorted.length;
    const res: string[][] = [];
    let prefix = "";
    for (let i = 0; i < searchWord.length; i++) {
        prefix += searchWord[i];
        let lo = 0, hi = n;
        while (lo < hi) {
            const mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;
            if (sorted[mid] < prefix) lo = mid + 1;
            else hi = mid;
        }
        const cur: string[] = [];
        for (let j = lo; j < Math.min(lo + 3, n); j++) {
            if (sorted[j].startsWith(prefix)) cur.push(sorted[j]);
            else break;
        }
        res.push(cur);
    }
    return res;
};
```

