traces.inference_trace:
The greedy two-pointer approach with backtracking to the last `*` solves this in O(n·m) worst case and O(1) extra space.

Pointers and state:
- `i` indexes `s`, `j` indexes `p`.
- `star` is the position of the last `*` seen in `p` (or -1 if none yet).
- `match` is the position in `s` where that `*` started matching.

Loop while `i < s.length`:
1. If `p[j]` is `?` or equals `s[i]`, advance both `i` and `j`.
2. Otherwise, if `p[j]` is `*`, record `star = j` and `match = i`, then advance `j`. This first tries letting `*` match the empty sequence.
3. Otherwise, if a `*` was seen before, backtrack: set `j = star + 1`, increment `match`, and set `i = match`. This lets that `*` absorb one more character.
4. Otherwise, there is no way to recover, so return false.

After `s` is consumed, skip any remaining `*` characters in `p`. The match succeeds only if `j` has reached the end of `p`.

```typescript
function isMatch(s: string, p: string): boolean {
    let i = 0, j = 0, star = -1, match = 0;
    const n = s.length, m = p.length;
    while (i < n) {
        if (j < m && (p[j] === '?' || p[j] === s[i])) {
            i++; j++;
        } else if (j < m && p[j] === '*') {
            star = j;
            match = i;
            j++;
        } else if (star !== -1) {
            j = star + 1;
            match++;
            i = match;
        } else {
            return false;
        }
    }
    while (j < m && p[j] === '*') j++;
    return j === m;
};
```

