traces.inference_trace:
Approach: a valid binary tree has exactly one root (a node with no parent), every other node has exactly one parent, and every node is reachable from the root.

1. **Count parents.** For each node `i`, look at `leftChild[i]` and `rightChild[i]`. For each child `c != -1`, increment `indeg[c]`. If any count goes above 1, a node has two parents, so return `false`.
2. **Find the root.** Collect the nodes with `indeg == 0`. If there is not exactly one such node, return `false`.
3. **Check reachability.** Run a BFS from the root and count the nodes visited. If we ever reach a node already seen, there is a cycle, so return `false`.
4. **Final check.** The tree is valid only if the BFS visited exactly `n` nodes.

Steps 1 and 2 alone are not enough. With one root and every in-degree ≤ 1, there can still be a separate cycle that the root never reaches. Step 3 catches this because the visited count falls short of `n`.

```typescript
function validateBinaryTreeNodes(n: number, leftChild: number[], rightChild: number[]): boolean {
    const indeg = new Array(n).fill(0);
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (const c of [leftChild[i], rightChild[i]]) {
            if (c !== -1) {
                indeg[c]++;
                if (indeg[c] > 1) return false;
            }
        }
    }
    let root = -1;
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (indeg[i] === 0) {
            if (root !== -1) return false;
            root = i;
        }
    }
    if (root === -1) return false;
    const visited = new Array(n).fill(false);
    const queue: number[] = [root];
    visited[root] = true;
    let count = 0;
    let head = 0;
    while (head < queue.length) {
        const node = queue[head++];
        count++;
        for (const c of [leftChild[node], rightChild[node]]) {
            if (c !== -1) {
                if (visited[c]) return false;
                visited[c] = true;
                queue.push(c);
            }
        }
    }
    return count === n;
};
```

