traces.inference_trace:
Use breadth-first search over words, where two words are adjacent if they differ in exactly one letter.

1. Put all of `wordList` into a set. If `endWord` is not in the set, no sequence exists, so return 0.
2. Start the BFS queue with `beginWord` at level 1.
3. For each word taken from the queue, try every position and every letter `a`–`z`.
   - If the new word equals `endWord`, return the current level + 1.
   - If the new word is in the set, delete it from the set (so it is visited only once) and push it onto the next level.
4. If the queue empties without reaching `endWord`, return 0.

Because BFS expands one level at a time, the first time `endWord` is reached gives the shortest sequence length.

Each word generates at most 10 × 26 candidates, so the work is about 5000 × 260 string builds, which is fast enough.

```typescript
function ladderLength(beginWord: string, endWord: string, wordList: string[]): number {
    const dict = new Set<string>(wordList);
    if (!dict.has(endWord)) return 0;
    dict.delete(beginWord);
    let queue: string[] = [beginWord];
    let level = 1;
    const L = beginWord.length;
    while (queue.length > 0) {
        const next: string[] = [];
        for (const w of queue) {
            const arr = w.split('');
            for (let i = 0; i < L; i++) {
                const orig = arr[i];
                for (let c = 97; c <= 122; c++) {
                    const ch = String.fromCharCode(c);
                    if (ch === orig) continue;
                    arr[i] = ch;
                    const nw = arr.join('');
                    if (dict.has(nw)) {
                        if (nw === endWord) return level + 1;
                        dict.delete(nw);
                        next.push(nw);
                    }
                }
                arr[i] = orig;
            }
        }
        queue = next;
        level++;
    }
    return 0;
};
```

